WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.100
SPEAKER: In our last
episode, we covered

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:05.250
best practices for deploying
and using Cloud Monitoring.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.860
Today, we'll start
looking at best practices

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:11.070
that you can use to get the
most value out of Cloud Logging.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:13.500
We'll cover how to
ingest and store logs.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.450
And we'll cover the
best ways to query logs

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:17.700
and manage logging costs
in our next episode.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.490
Welcome to Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:23.661
[MUSICAL INTRO]

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:27.100
Before we can do anything
with logs at scale,

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:28.650
our services need
to create them.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.920
And they need to be
ingested in Cloud Logging.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:33.900
We covered the sources
of logs in Google Cloud

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.807
in the previous episode,
but let's quickly review.

00:00:36.807 --> 00:00:39.390
There's nothing you need to do
to control how logs are emitted

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:41.490
from the platform and network.

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:43.350
But when writing
your own logs, there

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.900
are some best practices you
should follow to get the most

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.550
value out of that data.

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:50.220
This is where structured
logging comes in.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.520
Consider a log entry
that looks like this,

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.530
where the entire payload message
is in a single text payload

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:56.520
field.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.830
If you want to get any
data out of that field,

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:01.110
you have to use regular
expressions or other string

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:02.610
processing methods.

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:04.800
Using a structured
logging approach like this

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.070
to create log entries with a
JSON payload field that then

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:11.370
has nested fields under it
allows you to query and analyze

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:13.380
log data much easier.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:16.050
Every field within JSON
payload can be used to search

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:18.630
and query logs, just
like other fields.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:21.090
You can even add it to
the logs field explorer

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:24.502
to enable easily grouping and
searching by that field later.

00:01:24.502 --> 00:01:25.960
But how do you get
your application

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:27.650
logs to look like that?

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:30.370
If your logs are written by the
Cloud Logging or the Cloud Apps

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:32.680
agents, use one of
the supported parsers

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:35.020
to emit structured logs,
or write your own parser

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:36.280
if needed.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.590
If you're using the
Cloud Logging API,

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:42.190
send a full log entry
structure with a JSON payload.

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:44.590
Finally, if you're using
either the Cloud Logging

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:47.687
library or a third-party
logging library in your code,

00:01:47.687 --> 00:01:49.270
make sure you're
using them in the way

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:51.190
that emits structured logs.

00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:52.870
We'll link to the
documentation for each

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:55.335
of these in the episode notes.

00:01:55.335 --> 00:01:56.710
Writing logs in
a way that allows

00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:59.530
you to make the best use of
the data in them is great.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:02.560
But what's the best way to
store them to balance security,

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.690
retention, and access?

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:06.820
Let's have a look at
log centralization,

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:11.620
configuring retention, managing
access, and using exports.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:14.260
First, keeping your
logs in a central place

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:17.980
helps you manage all your logs
consistently and efficiently.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:19.510
We talked about
using log buckets

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:20.950
in the previous episode.

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:23.800
But remember that for each
Cloud project, billing account,

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:26.920
folder, and organization,
Cloud Logging automatically

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:30.790
creates two log buckets,
required and default.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:32.470
You can create
additional buckets

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:34.060
and configure log
sinks that send

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:37.090
logs from multiple
projects to those buckets.

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:40.920
It's often advisable to do this
for audit logs as an example.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.420
Your business needs
and requirements

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:45.420
will likely govern how long
you need to retain logs for.

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:47.040
You can configure
the retention period

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.260
for any user-defined
log bucket, and also

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:51.000
for the default log bucket.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.250
Simply edit the bucket
under log storage,

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:56.580
and set your preferred retention
period, up to 10 years.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:59.910
You can also control where the
logs in the bucket are stored.

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:02.280
You also have the option to
set the location to global

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:04.770
if you don't need to specify
where your logs are physically

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:06.360
stored.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.850
There are two mechanisms you
can use to control access

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:10.450
to logs in buckets.

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:13.090
The first one is
via IAM permissions.

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:15.390
We'll link to the detailed
guide in the episode notes,

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:17.730
but a user will at least
need the logging viewer

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:20.500
role to be able to read
logs in the project.

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.560
The second mechanism
to control log access

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:25.950
is log views, which provides you
with an advanced and granular

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:29.100
way to control access to the
logs in your log buckets.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:31.410
For example, consider
a scenario in which

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:33.000
you store all of
your organization's

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.130
logs in a central project.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:37.290
In this case, you
might want to control

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:40.260
which projects different
users can view logs from.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:42.840
Using custom log views, you
can give one user access

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.030
to logs only from
a single project

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:46.890
while you give another
user access to logs

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.120
from all the projects.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:50.980
Finally, you may
need to export logs

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:53.230
to a third-party
system such as Splunk.

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:55.300
You can do that using
a push-based method

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:57.430
through the Splunk
HTTP event connector

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:00.400
or a pull-based method, using
the Splunk add-on for Google

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.840
Cloud Platform.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:05.030
We covered best practices
for ingesting and storing

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:07.340
logs in Cloud Logging
and controlling log

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:09.865
access, retention, and exports.

00:04:09.865 --> 00:04:11.240
I hope these
recommendations help

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:13.790
you keep your services
reliable and your users happy.

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:15.740
In our next episode,
we'll tackle

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:18.709
best practices for analyzing
logs and managing logging

00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:19.310
costs.

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:21.029
Thanks so much for
watching today.

00:04:21.029 --> 00:04:23.360
Don't forget to like and
subscribe to never miss out

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.420
on more Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:27.020
See you soon.

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:30.370
[MUSICAL OUTRO]

